WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.800
JOANNA SMITH: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2019,

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.050
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.967
So if you didn't see
Migrating from a Monolith

00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:09.010
to Microservices,
then stay tuned

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.380
because here's the Rewind.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.855
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.070
What we've got
here is an overview

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.590
of what you could expect from
your own migration, brought

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.810
to you by two of
DevRel's finest.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.510
Adam Ross and Martin Omander
walk through the advantages

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.210
and some common hiccups
of refactoring a company's

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.220
monolithic infrastructure
into something that's

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.620
lighter, more agile,
and maintainable,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.020
thanks to microservices.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.070
Let's pause.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.810
What are microservices, anyway?

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.150
And does your system
qualify as a monolith?

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.710
Is that bad?

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.690
Well, I don't actually
know about your system.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.350
But a monolith system is
fully interconnected, meaning

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.840
every piece is part
of a single whole,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.020
where updating one feature
means rebuilding and redeploying

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.480
the entire system.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.190
As a contrast, microservices
are independently

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.980
built, deployed,
operated, and scaled.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.040
They connect to other services
by calling each other.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.680
But they are not
constructed or hosted,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.500
established-- whatever you
want to call it-- together.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.960
In the talk, Martin
explains that a microservice

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.200
has four defining
characteristics-- an API, which

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.380
is the only way to
communicate with the service;

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.680
compute resources to
run business logic;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.590
some sort of state-- it could
be database storage, a file

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.120
system, a memory cache,
whatever it needs.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.020
And most importantly,
according to Martin,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.150
a team that is
associated with it.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.100
Someone inside the
company to turn

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.470
to if something goes wonky.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.800
This leads to the core trade-off
that Adam and Martin discuss,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.710
development versus operations.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.380
Reducing technical
complexity makes it easier

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.780
to update and fix
your code but requires

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.420
more operational effort,
more deployments, harder

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.010
troubleshooting, et cetera.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.230
So the secret sauce
for these two?

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.514
Serverless.

00:01:53.514 --> 00:01:55.860
A serverless
approach handles most

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.780
of your operational
complexity, making

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.910
this trade-off irrelevant.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.270
And with microservices cutting
down on technical complexity,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.900
your company can move to
an agile, adaptable place.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.110
To prove that, Adam and Martin
had a complete microservice

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.920
introduced within 46 seconds
of their talk beginning.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.920
Sure, technically it was a
super simple Cloud Function.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.290
But that is still a
complete microservice

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.090
deployed with a one-liner.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.760
That is the agility they
are here to show off.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.860
So if I've caught
your interest, you're

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.230
going to want to see
the talk because they

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.150
managed to use a
fictional company's

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.490
journey as an iterative
demo that creates

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:36.570
a relatable migration story for
anyone who thinks microservices

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:40.020
are their future but may have
some concerns about getting

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.480
started, because while
these two are showing off

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.540
the power of Cloud
Functions, Cloud Run, Cloud

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.800
Firestore, Cloud Tasks,
and Google Sheets,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.550
they are also
calling out pitfalls

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.100
like data fragmentation
or component chains

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.930
scaling at different rates.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.925
All-around advice from two
people who have your back.

00:02:57.925 --> 00:02:59.550
And if you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.690
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.990
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.697
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:03:06.697 --> 00:03:07.530
Thanks for watching.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.580
